& [Need]
  + the Cantor repository and SOP preserve semantic authority but do not yet enumerate a clean-host selected runtime closure
  + the 410 GB development-build surface is replaceable sediment and cannot serve as the unique installation backup
  + later materialization cannot be audited until every expected runtime file has an exact producer source target prerequisite and receipt obligation

& [Decision]
  + form one pure platform-neutral runtime-closure graph compiler and independent verifier over supplied exact data
  + use a conservative path vocabulary deterministic graph normalization explicit unresolved prerequisites and an observation-free expected receipt

& [Rejected]
  + archiving all development targets hidden cache dependence mutable downloads ambient PATH embedded secrets installer effects provider/model acquisition and claiming installation from a compiled manifest

& [Consequence]
  + Cantor gains the compact semantic closure blueprint needed for later reinflation while all byte acquisition build installation activation and physical truth remain separately governed
